part of 'course_content.dart';

const extendedTopics = <Topic>[
  Topic("python-c1-t0", 1, "Comparisons and Boolean values", goal:"Compare values and distinguish Boolean results from assignment.", lesson:"A mock rule needs a clear yes/no result. Comparisons produce Boolean values: True or False. == asks whether values are equal; != asks whether they differ. <, <=, > and >= compare order. A single = assigns a value and is not a comparison.\n\nChoose types that match your question. The strings \"80\" and \"9\" are text; their ordering is not numeric ordering. Convert promised valid integer text before testing a numeric rule. Boolean literals use capital initials, and printing a Boolean produces True or False.\n\nA boundary belongs to one side of a rule. At least 18 includes 18; greater than 18 does not. Before coding, try one value below, exactly on, and above the boundary.\n\nComparison results can be returned from a function. A Boolean result is useful to a caller; a string such as \"True\" is text, not a Boolean. Our function tasks check the returned type as well as behaviour.\n\nAll access examples in this chapter are mock rules. A true comparison is not proof of real-world identity or permission. The next topic uses comparison results to choose branches.", example:"age = 18\nprint(age >= 18)\nprint(age == 18)\nprint(age > 18)", output:"True\nTrue\nFalse"),
  Topic("python-c1-t1", 1, "if, elif, and else", goal:"Choose one branch with if, elif and else.", lesson:"A reporting program may label a result differently depending on a value. An if statement evaluates a condition, and its indented body runs when that condition is truthy. An elif tests another condition only if earlier conditions in the same chain did not match. else covers the remaining cases.\n\nAn if/elif/else chain chooses at most one branch. Separate if statements are separate decisions and can both run. Order is important when conditions overlap: placing a broad condition before a stricter one can make the stricter branch unreachable.\n\nUse a colon after a branch header and consistent indentation for its body. A SyntaxError or IndentationError points to source structure. Check which statements belong to each branch before changing the rule.\n\nState every range in plain words and identify exact boundaries. In the example, high means at least 80; pass means at least 50 but below 80; review covers anything below 50. Inputs are promised to be valid integers so this task is about decisions, not conversion error handling.\n\nFor a function task, return the label rather than print it unless the question asks for output. The checker will call the function with several values. Next: combine conditions with and, or and not.", example:"score = 65\nif score >= 80:\n    print(\"High\")\nelif score >= 50:\n    print(\"Pass\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Review\")", output:"Pass"),
  Topic("python-c1-t2", 1, "and, or, and not", goal:"Combine Boolean conditions and explain short-circuit evaluation.", lesson:"A mock permission rule may need two conditions, either of two alternatives, or the absence of a blocking flag. and combines requirements, or combines alternatives, and not negates the truth value of its operand.\n\nFor Boolean values, A and B is true only when both are true. A or B is true when at least one is true. not A reverses a Boolean. In general Python, and/or return one of their operands rather than necessarily returning a bool; this topic's tasks use Boolean inputs and explicit Boolean results.\n\nEvaluation short-circuits: and skips the right side when the left is falsy; or skips the right side when the left is truthy. That can avoid evaluating an unnecessary expression. Parentheses make combined rules easier to read; use them to express the intended grouping instead of relying on remembered precedence.\n\nThe statement count >= 1 and count <= 3 can also be expressed as a chained comparison. Both endpoints are included. Test each requirement separately and then test their combinations.\n\nThe example is a mock rule, not an authentication system. In a real app, trusted server-side authorization must enforce access; a Boolean in this terminal cannot do that. Next: design a table of boundary cases before coding.", example:"has_badge = True\nis_suspended = False\nprint(has_badge and not is_suspended)\nprint(False or True)", output:"True\nTrue"),
  Topic("python-c1-t3", 1, "Boundary values and decision tables", goal:"Turn requirements into decision tables and boundary tests.", lesson:"Before implementing a decision, translate the wording into cases. A decision table records an input situation and the expected result. It is a plan you can inspect without running Python.\n\nLook for boundaries: exactly 0, just below 0, exactly a maximum, and just above it. Within a permitted range, test a typical value as well as endpoints. A finite set of tests cannot prove every possible program correct, but thoughtful cases expose common off-by-one mistakes and hardcoded examples.\n\nDistinguish invalid input from a valid negative result. In a capacity rule, a count outside the permitted numeric range may be Invalid; a valid count that uses less than the full capacity may be Available. Do not let a broad valid-looking branch absorb an invalid case.\n\nWrite a small function that returns the specified label. Testing a function separates the rule from input and display. The checker resets the environment for each case and calls your function with its own values.\n\nWhen zero is allowed in an arithmetic task, specify its result explicitly rather than silently divide by zero. This topic introduces a guard for that case. Next: nested conditions express decisions that depend on an earlier decision.", example:"count = 20\ncapacity = 20\nif count < 0 or count > capacity:\n    print(\"Invalid\")\nelif count == capacity:\n    print(\"Full\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Available\")", output:"Full"),
  Topic("python-c1-t4", 1, "Nested conditions", goal:"Use nested decisions when one rule depends on another.", lesson:"Sometimes a decision only makes sense after an earlier condition has been satisfied. For example, a mock login result may first ask whether the user is authenticated, and only then inspect a requested role. A nested if makes that dependency visible.\n\nIndentation tells Python which body owns a statement. Trace the outer condition first. If its branch does not run, neither do the nested decisions inside it. A rejected outer condition should not accidentally fall through to an allow message.\n\nAn early return can handle a rejecting case and reduce nesting. A nested version and an early-return version can both be correct if they obey the same contract. The checker accepts different valid structures; it does not require a copied source solution.\n\nList the meaningful combinations: not authenticated with any role, authenticated with an accepted role, and authenticated with an unaccepted role. Do not test only the role while ignoring the first requirement.\n\nThese are mock label functions, not a real account system. Real permissions belong in trusted application and database rules. In this academy, Supabase protects your saved workspace separately from your Python exercises. Next: truthiness and matching offer other ways to express decisions.", example:"authenticated = True\nrole = \"reviewer\"\nif authenticated:\n    if role == \"admin\":\n        print(\"Admin\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Member\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Sign in\")", output:"Member"),
  Topic("python-c1-t5", 1, "Truthiness and match statements", goal:"Use truthiness and literal match cases without hiding the rule.", lesson:"Python can use a value directly in a condition. False, None, numeric zero and empty strings or collections are falsy. A nonempty string is truthy—even \"False\" is nonempty text. If a task means exactly the Boolean True, do not silently treat arbitrary text as that Boolean.\n\nbool(value) produces a Boolean truth value. This topic uses simple strings, numbers and lists only to illustrate the rule; building larger collections comes later. Choose an explicit comparison when it states the requirement more clearly than a bare truthiness check.\n\nmatch compares a subject against patterns. For these introductory examples use literal text cases, and a final case _ for a fallback. Case order matters; the wildcard should not hide later cases. A bare name in a case is generally a capture pattern, not a comparison to a variable's existing value.\n\nThe example recognizes mock command labels only. It does not run commands on your computer. if/elif and match can both implement a small literal dispatch rule. Unless a task explicitly asks for a particular construct, correct equivalent behaviour is accepted.\n\nTo finish this chapter, combine cleanup, input validation and exact decision boundaries in a mock summary. Explain your cases separately from the program: passing output tests records local execution evidence, not a reviewed security qualification.", example:"command = \"status\"\nmatch command:\n    case \"status\":\n        print(\"Show status\")\n    case \"help\":\n        print(\"Show help\")\n    case _:\n        print(\"Unknown\")", output:"Show status"),
  Topic("python-c2-t0", 2, "while loops and termination"),
  Topic("python-c2-t1", 2, "for loops and range"),
  Topic("python-c2-t2", 2, "Lists and iteration"),
